  "title": "Les sons é, è et e muet",
  "subtitle": "é vs è, and the e that disappears",
  "icon": "✏️",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "pron-u06-l1",
      "kind": "pron",
      "title": "é and è",
      "goal": "Keep é closed and è open.",
      "tip": {
        "title": "é: smile and hold still; è: open your mouth",
        "body": "é (also -er, -ez, and et) is a short, tense vowel with spread lips — like the start of \"ay\" in \"day\" but without the glide to \"ee\". è, ê, -et and usually ai are more open, like \"e\" in \"bed\" (but j'ai and the future ending -ai, as in je serai, are é). Before a pronounced consonant you always use the open sound: mère, fête, belle. At the end of a word the contrast (pré/prêt) is clear in careful standard French, though many speakers make it less sharply in fast speech."
      },
      "phrases": [
        {
          "t": "Mon frère est né en été.",
          "n": "My brother was born in summer."
        },
        {
          "t": "Le café est prêt.",
          "n": "The coffee is ready."
        },
        {
          "t": "Elle préfère la mer.",
          "n": "She prefers the sea."
        },
        {
          "t": "Merci, c'est parfait !",
          "n": "Thanks, that's perfect!"
        }
      ],
